meta {
  name: Verify topic test event deleted (DELETE-then-GET-404)
  type: http
  seq: 67.5
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/events/{{topicDeletedEventCode}}?include=verify-deleted-{{$guid}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{authToken}}
}

tests {
  test("should return 404 after delete (regardless of 67's 204/404)", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(404);
  });

  test("cleanup: clear topic-event vars", function() {
    bru.setVar("createdEventCode", null);
    bru.setVar("topicDeletedEventCode", null);
    bru.setVar("topicDeleteStatus", null);
  });
}

docs {
  PR 6 (plan §D step 4 — "Audit DELETE assertions"). Pairs with
  67-cleanup-topic-test-event.bru. Whether 67 returned 204 (deleted now)
  or 404 (already gone from a prior failed run), the GET must return
  404 — anything else means the cleanup didn't actually take effect.

  ---

  Cache-bust query parameter (2026-05-29):
  Staging runs multiple ECS replicas of the event-management-service.
  EventController caches GET /events/{code} responses in a per-pod Caffeine
  cache keyed on `eventCode + "_" + include`, evicted via @CacheEvict on
  DELETE — but the @CacheEvict only fires on the pod that handled the
  DELETE. If a subsequent GET load-balances to a different pod, that pod's
  Caffeine cache still has the pre-delete entry and returns 200 → this
  test flakes red with "expected 200 to equal 404".

  Passing a unique include value per CI run (Bruno's $randomInt) lands a
  cache-key that no pod has cached, forcing every replica to fall through
  to the DB (where the event truly is gone) and return 404. The include
  itself doesn't add any resources to the response — we only care about
  the status code, and a 404 short-circuits before resource expansion.

  Proper fix lives in EMS (distributed-cache invalidation across pods,
  or stop caching at this layer) but is out of scope here — flagged for
  a follow-up. This Bruno-side patch unblocks the deploy-staging gate.
}
